[# Section 4 — identity, hashing, binary wire formats.
   Anchored in spec/canonical.md, spec/identity.md, spec/ast_bin.md,
   spec/data_bin.md. #]

[section id=identity n=4 title='Identity'

  [child id=canonical n=4.1 title='Canonical bytes'
    [intro
      """Two CX documents are equal iff their canonical bytes are equal.
         Canonical bytes are the unique serialization produced by `cx
         canon` — the rules are summarized in [[cx-canonical]] and stated
         normatively in [`spec/canonical.md`](../../spec/canonical.md)."""]

    [body
      """Why this matters: every distributed-systems primitive in CX —
         caching, deduplication, content-addressing, cross-binding equality
         — depends on byte-stable canonicalization. Without it, two
         "equal" documents might disagree on hash, on equality, or on
         what bytes get shipped over the wire. CX takes the same position
         as JCS for JSON, XCANON for XML, and the Nix store for files:
         there is one true serialization, and the rules to produce it are
         minimal and total."""]

    [list
      [item """**Attribute key order.** Lexicographic by attribute name.
              Ties broken by Unicode code point."""]
      [item """**String quoting.** Single quotes for single-line; triple
              double-quotes for multi-line. Escapes minimal — only what
              the parser requires."""]
      [item """**Whitespace.** No insignificant whitespace; bodies joined
              with single spaces; structural newlines only where the
              grammar requires them."""]
      [item """**Numeric form.** Integers as plain digits; floats in the
              shortest representation that round-trips to the same value
              (the standard IEEE-754 shortest-form rule)."""]
      [item """**Boolean form.** `true` / `false`. The sigil shortcuts
              `+name` / `-name` expand to canonical form."""]
      [item """**Comments stripped.** `[# … #]` is not part of canonical
              identity."""]]

    [example lang=cx
      """[# Source:                                                 #]
         [user :email 'ada@example.com' :id 42 +active]

         [# `cx canon` output (canonical bytes):                    #]
         [user :active true :email 'ada@example.com' :id 42]"""]]

  [child id=hash n=4.2 title='SHA-256 content hash'
    [intro
      """The identity of a CX document is the SHA-256 of its canonical
         bytes. The hash is computed over the UTF-8 byte sequence of the
         canonical form, not over the AST."""]

    [example lang=v
      """// V binding — Layer 1
         doc := cx.parse(source)!
         println(doc.hash())   // "3a7bd3e2360a..."""]

    [example lang=python
      """# Python binding — Layer 1
         doc = cx.parse(source)
         print(doc.hash())     # "3a7bd3e2360a..."""]

    [body
      """The hash function is stable across bindings: V, Python, Go, and
         Rust all produce identical strings for identical inputs. This is
         verified by [`conformance/binding_api.txt`](../../conformance)
         under gate 28.6 (Layer-1 parity)."""]

    [note
      """The hash is 64 hex characters. CX uses the full SHA-256, not a
         truncation, because storage cost is negligible and full hashes
         interoperate trivially with git, IPFS, Nix, and other
         content-addressable systems."""]]

  [child id=ast-bin n=4.3 title='ast-bin wire format'
    [intro
      """`ast-bin` is the binary serialization of a CX AST. It exists for
         in-process and cross-process transfer of parsed documents
         without re-parsing — bindings ship the AST over the C ABI rather
         than re-tokenizing text. Full spec in
         [`spec/ast_bin.md`](../../spec/ast_bin.md)."""]

    [body
      """ast-bin is **not** the canonical form. Canonical bytes are CX
         text. ast-bin is the **wire** form between processes that have
         agreed to skip parsing. Two ast-bin payloads can differ
         (different version, different option bits) and still represent
         the same canonical document. The hash is always taken over
         canonical text bytes, never over ast-bin."""]

    [list
      [item """**Versioned header.** Four-byte magic, format version,
              capability bits. A reader that sees an unknown cap bit
              refuses the payload."""]
      [item """**Capability bits.** Forward-compat advertisement.
              Currently bits 1–30 assigned (Path AST node and playground
              diagram use the most recent slots; v0.8.0 introduces
              PathNode-related bits). See
              [`spec/ast_bin.md`](../../spec/ast_bin.md)."""]
      [item """**Length-prefixed strings.** All names and string scalars
              are length-prefixed UTF-8."""]
      [item """**Varints for offsets.** Tree topology encoded with
              zig-zag varints."""]]]

  [child id=data-bin n=4.4 title='data-bin schema-driven format'
    [intro
      """`data-bin` is the binary value format for data — not parse
         structure. Where ast-bin carries AST nodes (with name / kind /
         child pointers), data-bin carries values (scalars, arrays, maps,
         tables) with optional schema-driven tag omission. Spec:
         [`spec/data_bin.md`](../../spec/data_bin.md)."""]

    [body
      """data-bin has two modes:"""]

    [list
      [item """**Self-describing.** Every value is tagged with its kind.
              Comparable to JSON or BSON in flexibility. Used when the
              schema is not known to both sides."""]
      [item """**Schema-driven.** Tag bytes and field names are omitted;
              the schema is the dictionary. Wire size comparable to
              Protobuf or Avro. Used for high-throughput bindings (e.g.
              the analytics-bridge Arrow / Parquet path)."""]]

    [body
      """The schema-driven mode is gated on capability bit (see
         [`spec/data_bin.md`](../../spec/data_bin.md) §3.13). A reader
         that does not advertise the schema-driven cap bit gets
         self-describing payload, never silently corrupted bytes."""]

    [body
      """Tables (`spec/data_bin.md` §3.10 + §3.11) get dictionary
         encoding, nullable column wrappers, and chunked row-group
         streaming. This is the wire format under the typed columnar
         table form in [[tables]]."""]]]
